## 1. Introduction

Last week I asked what Microsoft is worth. This week I am asking a different question: what is the price itself doing, and what might that say about what comes next? Technical analysis does not look at earnings or balance sheets. It looks at price and volume history and tries to read the behaviour of buyers and sellers from it.

The task asks for at least two stocks from different industries. I picked Microsoft and JPMorgan Chase. Microsoft is a technology company whose shares tend to be sensitive to interest rates and to expectations about AI spending. JPMorgan is the largest US bank, earning most of its money from lending, trading and investment banking, so its drivers are different. Both are huge and heavily traded, which tends to give cleaner charts than thinly traded shares do.

I should be clear about my method. I used public charting and data sites rather than my own charting software, so the indicator values below are the ones those sites published. Different sites calculate some indicators slightly differently, and I point out where that mattered. The figures I built are level maps made from reported price levels, not raw price charts.

