## 2. The Indicators I Used

| Indicator | What it measures | How I read it | Weakness |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Simple and exponential moving averages (50-day, 200-day) | Average closing price over the period. The exponential version weights recent days more. | Price above a rising 200-day average suggests a long-term uptrend. A 50-day average crossing above the 200-day is called a golden cross, and below it a death cross. | They lag. By the time a cross appears the move has usually happened. |
| Relative Strength Index, RSI (14) | Speed of recent gains against losses, on a scale of 0 to 100. RSI = 100 - 100 / (1 + average gain / average loss). | Above 70 is often called overbought and below 30 oversold. In strong trends it can stay high for weeks. | A high reading is not a sell signal on its own. |
| MACD (12, 26, 9) | The 12-day exponential average minus the 26-day, with a 9-day signal line. | MACD above its signal line is bullish momentum, below is bearish. The gap (histogram) shows whether momentum is building or fading. | Gives false signals when prices move sideways. |
| Support and resistance | Price areas where buying or selling has repeatedly appeared. | A break through resistance on heavy volume is a stronger signal than one on light volume. | Levels are judgement calls, not exact. |
| Fibonacci retracement | Common pullback depths, such as 38.2%, 50% and 61.8% of a prior move. | Pullbacks that stall near a retracement level are often watched as possible turning points. | Widely followed, but partly self-fulfilling and not scientific. |
| Volume | Number of shares traded. | Moves on rising volume carry more weight than moves on falling volume. | Needs context. Earnings days spike volume. |

*Table 2. Indicators and how I use them.*

