## 3. Chart Pattern Reference

Patterns are the shapes price tends to draw as buyers and sellers fight. I list the ones I looked for, how each is confirmed and what it usually implies. The "typical target" column is a rule of thumb used by chartists, and I treat it as a rough yardstick, not a prediction. None of these patterns works every time.

| Pattern | Type | What it looks like | Confirmation | Usual implication and rule-of-thumb target |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Head and shoulders | Reversal (bearish) | Three peaks, the middle one highest, with a roughly flat "neckline" through the two troughs. | Price closes below the neckline, ideally on higher volume. | Downtrend. Target is the neckline minus the height from head to neckline. |
| Inverse head and shoulders | Reversal (bullish) | The same shape upside down, after a downtrend. | Close above the neckline on higher volume. | Uptrend. Target is the neckline plus the head depth. |
| Double top | Reversal (bearish) | Two peaks at about the same level with a trough between them. | Close below the trough (support). | Downtrend. Target is the trough minus the peak-to-trough height. |
| Double bottom | Reversal (bullish) | Two lows at a similar level with a peak between. | Close above the middle peak. | Uptrend. Target is the peak plus the height of the pattern. |
| Ascending triangle | Usually continuation (bullish) | Flat resistance with rising lows pressing up against it. | Close above resistance on rising volume. | Further gains. Target is the breakout level plus the triangle's widest height. |
| Descending triangle | Usually continuation (bearish) | Flat support with falling highs pressing down. | Close below support. | Further losses. Target mirrors the ascending case. |
| Symmetrical triangle | Neutral | Highs falling and lows rising, converging. | Close outside either trend line. | Move in the direction of the breakout. Target is the widest height projected. |
| Bull flag or pennant | Continuation (bullish) | A sharp rise (the pole) followed by a small, tight pullback. | Break above the flag, often on a volume pick-up. | Another leg up. Target is the pole height added to the breakout. |
| Cup and handle | Continuation (bullish) | A rounded bottom followed by a small dip near the highs. | Close above the rim of the cup. | Uptrend resumes. Target is the cup depth added to the rim. |
| Rising or falling wedge | Reversal or continuation | Converging lines that both slope up (rising) or down (falling). | Break of the lower line (rising) or upper line (falling). | Rising wedges tend to break down, falling wedges up. |

*Table 3. Common chart patterns.*

**Figure 1** (chart in the Word version). Schematic examples of six common patterns. These are teaching illustrations, not real price data.

How do patterns influence prices? The honest answer is that they do not cause anything. They are a record of supply and demand. A head and shoulders matters because it shows buyers failing to make a new high on the third attempt, and traders who watch the same signal then sell in the same area. Patterns work, when they work, partly because many people react to them together. That is also why I look for confirmation, and why I never act on a pattern that has not broken out.

## 4. Stock One: Microsoft (MSFT)

### 4.1 The price story

Microsoft's chart over the last year has two very different halves. The shares set their highs in a $553 to $555 area during 2025. Then they fell about 37% to a 52-week low of $349.20 on 24 June 2026, even though the company's results were strong. A fall like that in a mega-cap is unusual, and Microsoft fell harder than other big technology names did over the same stretch. Coverage at the time pointed to worries about the cost of AI spending and its effect on free cash flow, which matches what I found in Week 2.

From that low the recovery has been sharp. The shares were back near $509 by 30 September, and on 13 October they closed at a record $543.45. On 16 October it fell 1.9% to $529.05. That leaves it 51.5% above the June low and 4.5% below the 52-week high.

### 4.2 Indicators

