### 4.5 Interpretation

The chart is constructive but late-stage in the short term. The long-term trend is up, the medium-term trend is up and momentum is positive. Against that, the price is close to a ceiling that has stopped it before, it sits more than 20% above its 200-day average, and the next catalyst is large. Microsoft reports on 28 October, and the Federal Reserve announces its decision the same day, with the 10-year Treasury yield near a two-decade high. Events like that can push a stock through a technical level in either direction in a single session.

## 5. Stock Two: JPMorgan Chase (JPM)

### 5.1 The price story

JPMorgan has been in an ascending channel since the April 2025 low of $202.16. It reached an all-time high of $366.50 on 13 August 2026, then pulled back about 11.1% to $325.87 on 1 October. That fall stalled almost exactly at two levels chartists watch closely: the 200-day moving average and the 61.8% Fibonacci retracement of the prior advance.

On 13 October the bank reported third-quarter results. Net income rose 6% to $15.3 billion, but diluted earnings per share of $5.57 (or $5.75 excluding a $658 million securities loss) fell short of estimates of roughly $5.9. It was the first miss since the fourth quarter of 2023. Investment banking fees were up 14% from a year earlier but down 8% from the prior quarter. The shares nevertheless held at about $336, helped by a better outlook for net interest income. I also noticed that, going into the report, bank shares generally were lagging: a major bank ETF was about 12% below its mid-August high while the S&P 500 and Nasdaq were at record highs.

